# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, which checks
# text files out with CRLF. That is harmless for .java and .yml, but it breaks
# load-tests/reset-fixtures.sh: bash reads the trailing \r as part of the
# command and fails with "$'\r': command not found". Pinning shell scripts to
# LF keeps them runnable on a fresh clone regardless of the cloner's settings.

* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
